## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend routing with governance constraints and to design testable prototypes.

## Key Concepts
- multi-module routing with governance
- signal design: intent, data_sensitivity, governance_policy
- edge-case handling: conflicts, missing role

## Code Example
```javascript
function route(prompt, signals){
  if (signals.governance_policy === 'restrict') return {module:'Data-Governance', payload:{prompt}};
  if (signals.data_sensitivity === 'PII') return {module:'PII-redactor', payload:{prompt}};
  return {module:'SQL-builder', payload:{prompt}};
}
module.exports = route;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit test the routing logic for edge cases like conflicting signals?
- How would you extend the router to support additional governance policies without breaking existing behavior?

## Why This Is Asked

Real-world fintech data assistants service multiple tenants; the router must enforce per-tenant policies, guard against data leakage, and handle conflicting signals and latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware policy retrieval and governance
- Guardrails and data-redaction
- Edge-case handling: conflicting signals, missing policy, leakage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-router sketch
async function routePrompt(prompt, tenantId){
  const policy = await fetchPolicy(tenantId);
  if(!policy) throw new Error('Missing policy');
  if(!policy.allow(prompt)) throw new Error('Action not allowed');

  const intent = analyzeIntent(prompt);
  const module = selectModule(intent, policy);
  const plan = generatePlan(module, intent);

  return { module, plan, payload: buildPayload(module, prompt) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design runtime policy enforcement and safe prompt routing under latency and drift.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime policy loading
- Injection detection and redaction
- Token budgeting and trimming
- Auditable decision logs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
async function buildPrompt(basePrompt){
  const policy = await fetch('http://localhost:8000/policy').then(r=>r.json());
  // injection guard
  let p = basePrompt.replace(/{{.*?}}/g, '');
  // secrets redaction
  p = p.replace(/(API_KEY|SECRET|PASSWORD)=[^\s]+/g, '[REDACTED]');
  // token cap (pseudo)
  const max = policy.maxTokens ?? 512; 
  return p.length > max ? p.slice(0, max) : p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test resilience to policy drift over time?
- What instrumentation would you add to monitor latency and false positives in production?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design guardrails early in prompt pipelines, focusing on simple heuristics, mitigations, and verifiable safety.

## Key Concepts

- Risk scoring from prompts
- PII and system-prompt leakage detection
- Mitigations: redaction, output limits, guardrails
- Guarded prompts and verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function riskScore(prompt) {
  let score = 0;
  if (/\b(email|e-mail|phone)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 3;
  if (/\b(export|download|CSV|exported)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 2;
  if (prompt.toLowerCase().includes("system prompt")) score += 4;
  return Math.min(score, 10);
}
function mitigate(prompt) {
  return prompt
    .replace(/[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g, "[REDACTED_EMAIL]")
    .replace(/\b\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}\b/g, "[REDACTED_PHONE]");
}
```

